[1mA real bug was found and fixed.[22m The Rust opened with [38;5;10mlet bw = unsafe { &mut *bw };[0m. Forming a reference from the caller's raw pointer asserts non-null, so a [38;5;10mNULL bw[0m became a Rust panic and [38;5;10mSIGABRT[0m, while the C simply faults with [38;5;10mSIGSEGV[0m. I changed [38;5;10msrc/lib.rs[0m to use raw place expressions ([38;5;10m(*bw).tot[0m, etc.), matching what the C does; this also drops a [38;5;10mnoalias[0m claim the C pointer never carried. Both libraries now fault identically.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
[1mGreen tests were not taken at face value.[22m [38;5;10mtests/phase_e_mutation_control.rs[0m injects nine plausible mistranslations — dropping [38;5;10m&= mask[0m, computing the loop guard in 64-bit instead of letting [38;5;10mbw->bits + bits[0m wrap as [38;5;10mu32[0m, clamping [38;5;10mb[0m instead of letting [38;5;10m63 - bw->bits[0m underflow, treating out-of-range shifts as yielding 0 instead of masking to 6 bits, saturating instead of wrapping, a signed ternary, skipping the loop, reordering the tail — and requires the same generators to catch every one. All nine are caught.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
That exercise surfaced a genuine property of this function: [1mevery loop iteration after the first is a no-op[22m, because [38;5;10mb = (tflac_u32)(63 - bw->bits)[0m drives [38;5;10mbw->bits[0m to exactly 63 (or clamps [38;5;10mbits[0m to 0) on iteration 1, after which later iterations only OR in bit 0, which [38;5;10m&= mask[0m immediately clears. So the [38;5;10mi < 100[0m cap value is unobservable. Rather than let that look like a coverage gap, [38;5;10mn4[0m asserts the equivalence for caps 1/101/10000 and [38;5;10mn5[0m confirms the cap is live code — if the reasoning were wrong, those tests fail.[0m[0m
